# Round 99: round 98 taught emit_val that a compiled function's NAME is not
# an env binding; three OTHER consumers of that same question were not
# told. Passing a function by name to a USER function emitted an env read
# that can only miss -- "undefined variable 'g'" at runtime, after
# arbitrary stdout, on programs the VM runs. Two independent wrongs, and
# the loud one was masking the quiet one: arg_class returned "unknown" for
# a function name so the callee's parameter defaulted NUMERIC, which would
# have stored a number where the VM stores a function.
#
# This is the callback convention the ecosystem actually uses --
# EigenRegex's ui handlers, liferaft's app_loop, lib/supervise's
# task_spawn, DMG's disasm_one all pass functions by name.
define g(x) as:
    return x + 1
define k(x) as:
    return x + 100
define stash(f) as:
    return 0
print of (stash of g)
define usebox(f) as:
    local box is [f]
    return ((box[0]) of 1)
print of (usebox of g)
tbl is {}
define reg(nm, f) as:
    tbl[nm] is f
    return 0
x is reg of ["a", g]
y is reg of ["b", k]
print of (tbl["a"] of 3)
print of (tbl["b"] of 3)
print of ((tbl["a"]) == g)
# (The fourth shape -- writing a function param into a numeric-classified
# module global -- is NOT pinned here: it dies loudly at runtime in the
# #148/#158 numeric-context family rather than silently, and closing it
# needs the param-class-flows-outward work. Filed as #173.)
print of "done"
